    "heroImage": "/images/doc_b20_7_img_1.jpg",
    "intro": "Soft, glowing sunrise hair color captures that dreamy moment when the sky shifts from dusky purple to golden light, and turns it into a wearable shade for everyday life. This look usually blends pinks, peaches, coppers, and warm blondes to mimic the horizon at dawn while still flattering your skin tone and haircut. Instead of flat, single-process color, sunrise hair depends on layered tones, soft transitions, and strategic placement so the color moves and shines when your hair does. It can be bold or subtle, depending on how bright you go with your reds, oranges, and yellows. Whether you love low-maintenance balayage or all-over fantasy color, sunrise-inspired shades can be tailored for short, medium, or long hair, and for every season.",
    "introParagraphs": [
      "Soft, glowing sunrise hair color captures that dreamy moment when the sky shifts from dusky purple to golden light, and turns it into a wearable shade for everyday life. This look usually blends pinks, peaches, coppers, and warm blondes to mimic the horizon at dawn while still flattering your skin tone and haircut. Instead of flat, single-process color, sunrise hair depends on layered tones, soft transitions, and strategic placement so the color moves and shines when your hair does. It can be bold or subtle, depending on how bright you go with your reds, oranges, and yellows. Whether you love low-maintenance balayage or all-over fantasy color, sunrise-inspired shades can be tailored for short, medium, or long hair, and for every season.",
      "Imagine your natural brown hair catching the first light of morning, with soft ribbons ofpeach, copper, and golden blondemelting through the mid-lengths and ends. Sunrise balayage on brown hair keeps your darker base at the roots, then gradually shifts into warm, sunlit tones that look dimensional but still natural. A colorist usually paints freehand highlights, focusing around the face and mid-lengths to mimic how the sun would naturally hit your hair. This approach works well if you want a noticeable change without the constant upkeep of full lightening from roots to ends. Ask for warm caramel and copper balayage pieces, then a peachy or rose-gold gloss over everything to tie it together. Regular toning every six to eight weeks keeps the sunrise effect bright without harsh lines."
    ],
